  "question": "Which process the object manager raises a handle's duplication in - pushed by its source, pulled by its target, or made between two others by a third - what its record names of the two ends, and where it raises the records of the handle a duplication made; and, machine-wide, where the records of the synchronization types' kernel handles are raised.",
  "answer": "In 4 runs of 16 rounds, machine-wide and nothing lost, each of the three duplications was recorded once, at its object's address, with the source's handle value, the target process and the handle it made there, and was raised in the process that asked for it: the creator's push of its event's handle in the creator, the opener's pull of the semaphore's in the opener, and the coordinator's duplication of the mutex's, between the two, in the coordinator. So a duplication's header names the process that asked for it - its source only when the source pushed - and its record names no source process. No duplication raised a creation of the handle it made, and each such handle's close was raised in its target, naming the object. Machine-wide, the synchronization types' kernel handles - their value's top bit set - were created in 1 to 8 processes' contexts and closed in 14 to 23, never the system process's, duplicated into the system process's table from 12 to 18, and the stop's rundown named the system process the holder of every one, 1,888 to 1,904, raising its records in 4 other processes' contexts.",
